        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Usman dan Fodio</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1754 CE – 1817 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">15 December 1754 in Maratta, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> of the Toronkawa clan, son of the scholar Muhammad Fodio and Hauwa bint Muhammad</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">20 April 1817 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>, aged 62</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Islamic scholar, teacher and writer; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a> (Amīr al-Muʾminīn), named by his followers in 1804; founder and first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">preaching tours in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, Kebbi and Zamfara from 1774; the community at Degel; the hijra to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudu">Gudu</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against Gobir; the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>; the education of women; more than a hundred books and many poems in Arabic, Fulfulde and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a></span></div>
