    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> family of scholars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, Usman dan Fodio preached for years among the peasants and herders of Hausaland, criticising its rulers for taxation, enslavement and what he judged to be un-Islamic practices.</li><li>When the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> moved against his community, he and his followers withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudu">Gudu</a> in 1804, named him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a> and fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> that by 1808 had defeated the rulers of Gobir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, Katsina and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms.</li><li>He handed the running of the new state to his son Muhammed Bello and his brother Abdullahi, and returned to teaching and writing; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> outlasted him by almost a century, until the British conquest.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Usman dan Fodio held that women should receive the same education as men and put them in the same classrooms; several of his daughters became scholars and writers, and one of them, Nana Asmaʼu, translated some of his work into local languages.</p></aside>
    </section>

