    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1754</span><span class="rf-text">Usman is born in Maratta, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> family of the Toronkawa clan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1774</span><span class="rf-text">He begins itinerant preaching, first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> and Kebbi and later in Zamfara</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1789</span><span class="rf-text">A mystical vision leads him to believe he has the power to work miracles and to teach his own litany, or wird</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1802</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>, the new king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> and once Usman's student, revokes the autonomy of Degel and tries to have him killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1804</span><span class="rf-text">Usman and his followers leave Degel for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudu">Gudu</a> and name him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> begins with the victory at Tabkin Kwotto</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1808</span><span class="rf-text">By this year Usman has defeated the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, Katsina and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1812</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>'s administration is reorganised, with Muhammed Bello and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullahi-dan-fodio">Abdullahi dan Fodio</a> governing east and west</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1817</span><span class="rf-text">Usman dies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> and his son Muhammed Bello succeeds him as the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello.jpg" alt="Ahmadu Bello" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello">Ahmadu Bello</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Ahmadu_Bello_(1959).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eliot Elisofon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Usman dan Fodio was born on 15 December 1754 in Maratta, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>. His name means “son of Fodiye”, his father, Muhammad Fodio, having earned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> title Fodiye, “the learned”. The family were Toronkawa, descended from scholars who had moved into Hausaland from Futa Tooro in the 1300s; his father later moved them to Degel. His mother, Hauwa, came from a line of literary scholars, and she and his father were his first teachers. His grandmother Ruqayyah was a respected ascetic and teacher whose work was popular among scholars of the 18th and 19th centuries. Family tradition, repeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadu-bello">Ahmadu Bello</a>, holds that Hauwa descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idris-i-of-morocco">Idris I of Morocco</a>.</p><p>He studied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> with his father and then with other teachers, among them his relatives Uthman Bn Duri and Muhammad Sambo, and then Jibril bin Umar, a leading religious figure who supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a>. Jibril later fell out with him over how a Muslim should be defined. At the age of 20 Usman set up a school in Degel, where he taught and preached. In 1786 he attended the assembly of his cousin Muhammad bn Raji and received further certification. By 1787 he was writing books in Arabic and long poems in Fulfulde.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/nana-asmau">Nana Asma'u<span>Child</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bello">Muhammad Bello<span>Child</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-atiku">Abu Bakr Atiku<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1774 Usman began preaching as an itinerant mallam. He preached for twelve years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> and Kebbi and then for five years in Zamfara; his students included his younger brother Abdullahi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>, a future king of Gobir. He condemned the ruling elite for enslavement, idol worship, sacrificial rituals, overtaxation, arbitrary rule and greed, insisted on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of law in worship, commerce and criminal law, and denounced pagan customs and inheritance practices that denied women their shares. His following grew among peasants and the lower classes, among slaves, and among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuareg">Tuareg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> herders whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> officials had seized.</p><p>He broke from the royal court and founded a religious community at Degel, where he stayed about twenty years. In 1789 a vision led him to teach his own litany, and in the 1790s he had visions of Abdul Qadir Gilani and was initiated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qadiriyya">Qadiriyya</a>, of whose brotherhood he later became head. Around 1788–89 the ageing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bawa-jan-gwarzo">Bawa of Gobir</a> summoned him to Magami and granted five concessions, among them freedom for prisoners and respect for those who wore the turban of his community. In 1797–98 Nafata, Bawa's successor, reversed this policy, banning preaching, turbans and veils, and conversions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>, who had succeeded Nafata, revoked Degel's autonomy and tried to have Usman killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alkalawa">Alkalawa</a>. When a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> force returning with Muslim prisoners passed Degel and was challenged, Yunfa's forces attacked Gimbana. Usman wrote that Muslims under persecution must emigrate, and in 1804 his community left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudu">Gudu</a>, a journey of four or five days; Muslims from across Hausaland joined them. His followers named him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a>, with Abdullahi first and Muhammed Bello second to give the salute of allegiance. At about fifty he was old and weak and took no part in the fighting.</p><p>The first major battle, Tabkin Kwotto in June 1804, was won by Abdullahi against the larger Gobir force. At Tsuntsua, two miles from the Gobir capital, the Muslims lost about 2,000 men, 200 of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> memorisers, according to Bello; they then took Kebbi and fought off a joint attack at Alwassa, where Bello recorded about a thousand dead. Usman appointed fourteen leaders to take the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> to their own regions. By 1808 he had defeated the rulers of Gobir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a>, Katsina and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kingdoms. In 1812 Bello and Abdullahi were given the western and eastern governance, and Usman returned to teaching and writing. Judges, market inspectors and prayer leaders were appointed, and an Islamic tax and land system was set up.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Usman's chief opponents were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a> kings Bawa, Nafata and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunfa">Yunfa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullahi-dan-fodio">Abdullahi dan Fodio</a>'s account, written at the Shehu's insistence, says the king of Gobir terrorised his followers, sold captured men, women and children into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> before their eyes and ordered Usman to leave without his followers; it is the account of one side. The sultans of Hausaland were, in Usman's own words in Tanbih al-ikhwan, “undoubtedly unbelievers, even though they may profess the religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>”. After Kwotto many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> kings and chiefs launched a strong assault on Muslim communities in their territories, and a commercial embargo was placed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudu">Gudu</a>.</p><p>Within the learned world he had other opponents. Jibril bin Umar disagreed with him over who counts as a Muslim, and Elkanemi of Borno objected to his education of women, to which Usman responded in writing. The motives of his allies were also questioned. According to Bello, the friendship of the Zamfara leaders came from their hostility to Gobir rather than from adherence to Islam, and the villagers were unwilling to sell the Muslims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>. Murray Last holds that the campaigns can be explained as a search for food, pasture and water for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the reorganisation of 1812 Usman gave his time to teaching and writing about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and to building a government grounded in Islamic law. In 1815 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a>, where Bello built him a house in the western suburbs. He died there on 20 April 1817, at the age of 62. Gidado dan Laima listed two wives, his first cousin Maymuna, with whom he had 11 children including the twins Hasan and Nana Asmaʼu, and Aisha, known as Gaabdo and Iyya Garka, who was famed for her Islamic learning and outlived him by many decades.</p><p>His son Muhammed Bello succeeded him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Commander of the Faithful</a> and became the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the Sokoto Caliphate, and Abdullahi was given the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwandu-emirate">Gwandu</a> and charge of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>. All the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa states</a>, parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nupe-kingdom">Nupe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> outposts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauchi">Bauchi</a> and Adamawa were then ruled by one political and religious system. By 1830 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> had engulfed most of what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. From his time until the British conquest at the beginning of the 20th century there were twelve caliphs.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Usman the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> expanded into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, southern Niger and most of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, and at the time it was the largest state south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. It combined an Islamic state with a modified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> monarchy. David Westerlund described the result as a federal theocratic state, with extensive autonomy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirates</a>, which recognised the spiritual authority of the caliph. The nomadic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> herders were settled and mosques and schools were built; Arabic, Hausa and Fulfulde poetry revived, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was taught in Hausa and Fulfulde.</p><p>Usman wrote more than a hundred books, on law, government, society and religion, and many poems; estimates of his poems range from 118 to about 480. His litanies are still widely practised. Many Muslims regard him as a mujaddid, a divinely sent renewer of the faith, and the Encyclopedia Britannica has treated him as the most important reforming leader of the 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-sudan">Western Sudan</a>. His movement followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihads</a> of Futa Bundu, Futa Tooro and Fouta Djallon, and inspired later ones, among them those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a>, who founded the Massina Empire, and Omar Saidou Tall, founder of the Toucouleur Empire, who married one of his granddaughters.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Usman dan Fodio (1754–1817), a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> scholar from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gobir">Gobir</a>, preached for years against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> rulers, withdrew with his followers in 1804 and led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> that by 1808 had defeated Gobir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kano">Kano</a> and Katsina. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>, left its government to his son Muhammed Bello and his brother Abdullahi, and wrote more than a hundred books.</em></p>

